 "version": "storyboard.v3",
 "profile": "DC",
 "topic": "What Kubernetes actually does",
 "entities": {},
 "sentences": [
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "text": "Everyone runs Kubernetes, but what does it actually do?"
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "text": "Picture three hundred containers spread across forty machines."
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "text": "Machines crash, traffic spikes, and new versions ship every hour."
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "text": "Doing that by hand means someone is always awake at three in the morning."
  },
  {
   "id": "s5",
   "text": "Kubernetes flips the job around: you describe what you want, not how to get it."
  },
  {
   "id": "s6",
   "text": "Three replicas of the checkout service, each with two gigabytes of memory."
  },
  {
   "id": "s7",
   "text": "The scheduler reads that wish and finds nodes with room for every copy."
  },
  {
   "id": "s8",
   "text": "Then a control loop compares what you asked for with what is running."
  },
  {
   "id": "s9",
   "text": "When node seven dies, two replicas vanish with it."
  },
  {
   "id": "s10",
   "text": "The loop notices the gap within seconds and starts two fresh copies elsewhere."
  },
  {
   "id": "s11",
   "text": "Nobody gets paged; the cluster simply heals back to three."
  },
  {
   "id": "s12",
   "text": "That is the whole idea: declare the goal, and let a loop keep it true."
  }
 ],
